This directly addresses the task: comparing two pescatarian diet approaches for the purpose of eating healthier.

ANSWER: {"answer": "I visited Healthline (healthline.com), searched for 'pescatarian diet', and opened the article 'Pescatarian Diet: What to Eat and Avoid, Benefits, and More' (healthline.com/nutrition/pescatarian-diet). The article explains that not all pescatarian diets are equal, and compares two versions for healthiness: 1) A whole-food, balanced pescatarian diet — built on whole grains, legumes, nuts, seeds, fruits, vegetables, dairy/eggs, and fish/shellfish as the main protein, which provides omega-3s (EPA/DHA from oily fish like salmon and sardines), lean protein, and micronutrients (B12, zinc, selenium from seafood like oysters, mussels, cod), and is linked to lower risk of obesity, diabetes (4.8% vs 7.6% in omnivores), and heart disease (22% lower death risk) versus a meat diet. 2) A processed/low-quality pescatarian diet — technically meat-free and fish-inclusive, but relying on processed starches, junk food, and fried items like fish sticks instead of whole foods, which carries the common vegetarian-diet pitfall of excess refined carbs and loses most of the health benefits of the first approach. The article's conclusion is that for 'eating healthier' as a pescatarian, the whole-food-based version with fish as a key protein source (preferably baked/grilled/sautéed rather than fried, with moderation of high-mercury fish like swordfish/shark/king mackerel) is the healthier of the two, while the processed/fried-fish version offers little advantage over a typical omnivore diet despite excluding red meat and poultry. No sign-in, account creation, or personal data entry was needed or performed."}

